  dir: "chip",
  category: "atoms",
  overview: [
    "A Chip is a LOW-emphasis tag, not a call to action: the neutral chip is a 28 px outlined pill (the raised neutral fill inside a hairline, its label at weight 500). A status chip is its soft pill (the status wash under the status ink, the recipe Alert and the other toned surfaces share); a palette hue is that hue's 16% wash inside the same hue at 30%, under a deep ink in the hue that holds 4.5:1 on the wash. A selectable chip rests as the bare pill in the field border, and once selected it is the accent's soft wash in the accent ink, inside an accent edge and leading with a check. The chip owns that whole look, so no call site hand-composes a `borderRadius` + `backgroundColor` + padding Pressable.",
    "Two orthogonal axes drive the look:",
    { list: ["**Color** (pick one; default the neutral tag). A semantic status (`success`, `warning`, `destructive`, `info`, `neutral`) or a free-form palette hue (`red`, `orange`, `amber`, `yellow`, `lime`, `green`, `emerald`, `teal`, `cyan`, `sky`, `blue`, `indigo`, `violet`, `fuchsia`, `purple`, `pink`, `rose`, `gray`). Status names read the theme's status colors (`statusColors`), so a success chip matches a success Badge and Alert; `info` is the primary color. Precedence when more than one is set: status names first, then hues in the order above.","**Emphasis**. `outline` drops the fill for the field-border edge around the color's ink; `primary` is the solid accent; `secondary` is the higher raised fill with no edge. `neutral` is the muted tag with no edge; `gray` is the same muted fill and ink inside the chip's own edge. `outline` composes with any color, e.g. `<Chip blue outline>`. Precedence: a color wins over `primary`, and `outline` wins over `primary`'s fill."] },
    "`count` adds a dimmed trailing count in the mono face. A hovering pointer brightens the chip on the web. `start` pins a chip when its host layout does not publish a Canvas sizing context.",
    "`steadyPress` retains the resting opacity during a press, while selection, hover feedback and the native ripple keep working.",
    "`href` makes the chip a link to a route or a URL: a real anchor on the web (so middle-click and open-in-new-tab work and assistive tech hears a link), with `hrefAttrs` forwarding `target`, `rel` and `download`. Native platforms have no anchors, so pair `href` with an `onPress` that navigates through the app's router; it receives the press event, so a web app routing on the client calls `event?.preventDefault()` there. A hovering pointer turns a link chip's ink and edge to the accent instead of brightening it. A link is not a toggle: `href` is ignored on a selectable chip or a ChipGroup option.",
  ],
